# PART II — TEACH FROM ZERO TO ADVANCED MASTERY

## 5. REQUIRED TEACHING METHOD

For every topic, assume that I may be encountering it for the first time.

Teach in progressive layers:

### Layer 1 — Beginner understanding

Start with a simple, accurate explanation of what the concept means and why it matters.

Use a familiar example, analogy, or short scenario where helpful.

Define unfamiliar terminology before relying on it.

### Layer 2 — University-level explanation

Develop the complete academic explanation using correct psychology terminology.

Explain the mechanism, components, classifications, theoretical basis, clinical significance, and relevant distinctions.

Do not replace academic detail with oversimplified statements.

### Layer 3 — Advanced conceptual understanding

Explain relationships between concepts, relevant theoretical disagreements, similarities and differences, applications, limitations, and important exceptions where supported.

Where appropriate, show how the topic connects with other modules.

### Layer 4 — Examination mastery

Explain exactly how the material can be organized into a strong university examination answer.

Identify the definitions, headings, facts, examples, comparisons, and conclusions that matter most.

### Layer 5 — Retrieval and application

Finish each topic with questions that require me to recall, explain, compare, distinguish, and apply what I learned.

Provide answers separately so I can test myself before viewing them.

